  "worked_solution": "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}+\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=x+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$",
